RULE NUMBER
012

TITLE
Forge: Generate Terraform module (tf/) from proto definitions

ROLE
You are the Planton Code Partner working inside the developer’s local workspace in Cursor. This rule scaffolds the Terraform module for a resource under the component-root `iac/tf/`.

TOOLS
- Proto Readers: reuse readers for `api.proto`, `spec.proto`, `input.proto`, `outputs.proto`.
- Variables Generator: `_rules/component/_scripts/terraform_module_write.py` (run with `--make-local --generate-variables`)
- Module Writer: `_rules/component/_scripts/terraform_module_write.py`
- Reference components (live ground truth): `catalog/aws/awsvpc/iac/tf/`
  and `catalog/aws/awsalb/iac/tf/` -- canonical `variables.tf`/`provider.tf`/`locals.tf`/`main.tf`/`outputs.tf` layout.

PARITY WITH PULUMI (MANDATORY -- read before writing any HCL)
Terraform is authored AFTER the Pulumi module in the sequence, so the Pulumi module
(`iac/pulumi/module/*.go`) already exists and is the thing to reconcile against. This rule's
job is to produce a Terraform module that is at **100% behavioral parity** with it -- the
same cloud objects, names, labels, selectors, environment, dependency ordering, and stack
outputs for an identical `stack-input`. Neither engine is privileged: the proto contract
(`spec.proto` + `outputs.proto`) and intended behavior decide correctness, so if the
Pulumi module is the one that is wrong, fix it too. Do not author Terraform "from the proto"
in isolation and hope it lines up; author it by reconciling against the existing Pulumi
module field by field.

Before drafting, OPEN the sibling Pulumi module and walk the parity-dimension checklist in
`pkg/iac/MODULE_PARITY.md`. Confirm your Terraform matches Pulumi on each:
- **Namespace / scope source** -- same spec field (e.g. `var.spec.namespace`), never a
  derived name or `metadata.id`.
- **Resource naming basis** -- created objects (operator CRs, secret names, pod annotations)
  key off the SAME field Pulumi uses (`metadata.name` is the established basis). A common
  drift is keying off a `resource_id` local instead.
- **Labels** -- identical keys AND values to `locals.go` (the `kuberneteslabelkeys` set;
  the kind value is the `CloudResourceKind` enum string, NOT an ad-hoc string like
  `"postgres_kubernetes"`; conditional labels appear under the same condition).
- **Selectors** -- pod/service selectors must match the labels the operator/chart actually
  applies to workload pods (e.g. Zalando/Spilo pods are `application: spilo`), exactly as
  Pulumi selects. A selector built from resource-identity labels matches zero pods.
- **Spec feature coverage** -- every field in `spec.proto` that Pulumi honors is honored
  here too (backup, restore/standby, ingress, env injection, sizing, ...). A field handled
  in `module/*.go` but absent from `*.tf` is a parity defect, even if `terraform validate`
  passes.
- **Dependency ordering** -- the same edges Pulumi expresses via `pulumi.DependsOn`
  appear here via `depends_on` / `metadata.relationships`.
- **Outputs** -- `outputs.tf` must emit shapes that flatten onto the SAME
  `outputs.proto` fields Pulumi exports. Nested proto messages are emitted as nested
  HCL objects (`output "x" { value = { name = ..., key = ... } }`), which the generic
  transformer flattens to `x.name` / `x.key`. NEVER emit flat names like `x_name` -- they
  never reach the nested proto field.

DEVIATION POLICY: the default is exact parity. Deviate ONLY for a strong technical reason
(a provider feature absent from one SDK, an operator that names a thing differently). When
you must, keep the divergence minimal, ensure it does NOT change the stack outputs, and add
a `# PARITY-EXCEPTION:` comment here AND a matching `// PARITY-EXCEPTION:` in the Pulumi
module stating what differs and why. An undocumented divergence is a defect.

SEQUENTIAL STEPS
1) Read: Use proto readers to derive `<Kind>`, spec, outputs, and provider context.
   ALSO read the sibling Pulumi module (`iac/pulumi/module/*.go`) -- it is the existing
   engine to reconcile against for everything below (correct it if it is the wrong one;
   neither engine is privileged).

   FAST PATH -- Kubernetes-CRD-projection kinds: If the kind's spec is a direct
   1:1 projection of a single Kubernetes custom resource (Istio, Gateway API,
   etc.) AND the kind is annotated with `kubernetes_manifest_projection` in
   `CloudResourceKindMeta` (cloud_resource_kind.proto), the ENTIRE module is
   deterministic -- do NOT hand-author it. Generate it in one shot:
   `echo '{"files":[]}' | python3 _rules/component/_scripts/terraform_module_write.py --provider <provider> --kindfolder <kindfolder> --kind <Kind> --stdin --make-local --generate-module --validate | cat`.
   This emits variables.tf (spec/metadata typed `any`), a thin locals.tf, a
   kubernetes_manifest main.tf (GVK from the annotation), provider.tf, and
   outputs.tf. The proto->tfvars converter emits the camelCase, null-pruned spec
   at runtime, so there is no snake->camel / null-prune / oneOf logic to write.
   Then STOP -- skip steps 2-4 below.

   Do NOT use this path for kinds whose Planton spec ABSTRACTS over the CRD
   (e.g. cert-manager's issuer_ref oneof -> issuerRef.kind/name, or any CRD with
   a required field whose valid value is the empty string, which protojson omits
   -- e.g. ReferenceGrant's to[].group). Those keep the hand-written path below.

2) Generate variables and read it: `echo '{"files":[]}' | python3 _rules/component/_scripts/terraform_module_write.py --provider <provider> --kindfolder <kindfolder> --kind <Kind> --stdin --make-local --generate-variables | cat`. Then open the generated `variables.tf` in the module path.
3) Draft: Consult the reference component `iac/tf/` directories. CAUTION when
   mirroring a sibling's `variables.tf` instead of using the generator: the
   proto->tfvars converter flattens every `StringValueOrRef` field to a PLAIN
   STRING before the module runs (`pkg/iac/tofu/generators/typerules.go`), so a
   ref field must be typed `string` in variables.tf — some older modules carry a
   stale `object({ value = string })` shape that fails at plan time with
   "attribute ...: object required". The generator emits the correct shape;
   trust it over any hand-written sibling. The same converter DROPS every spec
   field marked `(dev.planton.shared.options.manifest_only)` before the module
   runs (a manifest word such as a selector's `match_all` or an access config's
   `ephemeral`, whose wire spelling is the absence of its siblings), and the
   generator declares no variable for it -- so a module never declares, reads,
   or forwards such a field; it renders the shape the field's validation rules
   already guarantee (a selector with no criteria, a config with no static
   address). Prepare a files JSON manifest for non-generated files. Comment the HCL to the module-comment bar (`architecture/component.md` §4.1 Code Quality): explain *why*, trade-offs, provider quirks, and non-obvious ordering -- not line-by-line narration. These modules render on the public catalog, so their comments are part of the deliverable.
4) Write and validate:
   - `cat <<'JSON' | python3 _rules/component/_scripts/terraform_module_write.py --provider <provider> --kindfolder <kindfolder> --kind <Kind> --stdin --validate | cat`
     { "files": [ {"name": "provider.tf", "content": "..."}, {"name": "locals.tf", "content": "..."}, {"name": "outputs.tf", "content": "..."} ] }
     JSON
   - If validation fails, refine and re-run (≤3).
5) Reconcile parity (MANDATORY): re-walk the PARITY WITH PULUMI checklist above against the
   finished module and confirm each dimension matches the Pulumi module. Verify outputs with
   the automated guard rather than by eye:
   `planton validate-outputs --kind <Kind> --module-dir catalog/<provider>/<kind>/iac/tf --sample-outputs <sample>.json`
   (expect full proto population, zero unmapped). Resolve every mismatch now, or record it as
   a documented `PARITY-EXCEPTION:` in both modules.

OUTPUT
- Show variables generation outcome (path/exit code) and confirm `variables.tf` was read.
- Show created/updated file list and key JSON fields from the writer tool.
- Show validate results.
- Report the parity reconciliation outcome: "parity achieved" or the explicit list of
  documented `PARITY-EXCEPTION:` divergences.

SUCCESS CRITERIA
- `variables.tf` is generated in step 2 at the module path and matches the expected schema
- Drafted files reference inputs that exist in `variables.tf`
- Core files (`provider.tf`, `locals.tf`, `outputs.tf`, resource files) exist and `terraform validate` passes
- Module code is richly commented to the module-comment bar (why/trade-offs/provider quirks/ordering, not narration)
- **Cross-engine parity holds:** every parity dimension matches the Pulumi module, or the
  divergence is minimal, output-neutral, and carries a `PARITY-EXCEPTION:` comment in BOTH
  modules; `planton validate-outputs` reports full proto population with zero unmapped outputs

NOTES
- Generate variables once up-front; derive convenience in `locals.tf`.
- **Offline plans through the CLI must point at the LIVE module with an ABSOLUTE
  `--module-dir`:** `planton tofu plan --manifest <abs path> --module-dir <abs
  path to catalog/<provider>/<kind>/iac/tf>`. Without `--module-dir` (or when the working directory
  is not the module dir) the CLI silently falls back to a STAGING CLONE of the
  repo — a stale checkout whose `variables.tf` predates your edits — and the
  plan fails with schema errors that look like your module's ("attributes ...
  are required") but come from code you never wrote. A RELATIVE `--module-dir`
  also fails (the generated tfvars path resolves against the wrong root). Both
  paths absolute, always.
- **Variable validations on nullable optional objects must guard with `try()`:**
  HCL's `||` evaluates BOTH operands (it does not short-circuit), so a validation
  shaped `var.spec.block == null || length(var.spec.block.field) > 0` fails at
  plan time with "Attempt to get attribute from null value" whenever the block is
  omitted. Write `var.spec.block == null || length(try(var.spec.block.field, {})) > 0`
  instead. This only bites when the block is absent, so it escapes a happy-path
  `terraform validate` against a fully-populated manifest — test the omitted case.
- **Never guard possibly-empty optional strings with `coalesce()`:** HCL's
  `coalesce()` skips empty strings as well as nulls, so `coalesce(var.spec.name, "")`
  ERRORS (no non-empty argument) when the field is legitimately `""` — and inside a
  validation's `can(...)` that error silently becomes `false`, failing the variable
  with a misleading message. For an `optional(string, "")` field write the guard as
  `var.spec.name == "" || can(regex(...))` (or `try(var.spec.name, "") == ""`).
  This bites exactly when the optional field is omitted, so it escapes a plan
  against a fully-populated manifest — test the omitted case.
- **Gate ambient-project data sources with `count` on actual need:** an
  unconditional `data "google_project"` (or any live-lookup data source) executes
  during EVERY plan — including offline plans against the e2e manifest's sample
  project — and fails with "user does not have permission to access Project".
  When a module resolves the provider's effective project only for entries that
  omit theirs, compute a `needs_ambient_*` local (spec project empty AND some
  entry empty) and set `count = local.needs_ambient_* ? 1 : 0` on the data
  source, referencing it as `data.google_project.this[0]`. This also mirrors the
  Pulumi side's only-when-needed `GetClientConfig` lookup, keeping the engines'
  resolution behavior identical.
- **Outputs surfacing provider-`Sensitive` attributes MUST carry `sensitive = true`.**
  OpenTofu rejects the whole configuration at plan time ("Output refers to
  sensitive values") when a root output references any attribute the provider
  schema marks `Sensitive` -- and sensitivity propagates through `try()`,
  `split()`, and index expressions. The trap: name-like fields can be
  sensitive too (azurerm marks the whole `site_credential` block sensitive,
  including its `name`, and `custom_domain_verification_id` on App Service
  kinds). Check the provider schema's `Sensitive:` flag for every attribute an
  output touches -- `terraform validate` does NOT catch this class; only a
  plan does.
- **Provider budgets on module-derived values are `lifecycle { precondition }`
  blocks on the resource, twins of the Pulumi module's guard.** When a service
  caps something the module itself assembles (a derived name, the composed tag
  set including the Planton labels), the author cannot see the final value and
  no spec rule can compute it, so the module checks it before anything renders:
  a `local.<thing>_budget` naming the measured number (comment carries the date
  and the API's own error text), a local computing the FINAL value sent (after
  `distinct()`, after label rendering), and a precondition whose
  `error_message` interpolates the budget, the offending total, and the
  author's levers -- the same number and the same words as the Pulumi module's
  `errors.Errorf`. Measure the boundary live (pass at N, fail at N+1) before
  writing the number. Exemplars: the Superset chart's name budget; the
  DigitalOcean database cluster's 255-character combined-tags budget.
- **Dispatch-pattern outputs coalesce PER ATTRIBUTE, never per resource.**
  When one kind count-gates between several provider resources (an
  exactly-one-of scope or mode discriminator selecting among variants with
  identical faces), splice the outputs attribute-by-attribute
  (`coalesce(try(variant_a[0].id, null), try(variant_b[0].id, null), ...)`).
  Coalescing whole resource OBJECTS taints every derived output with the
  union of the object's sensitivity -- a resource carrying any `Sensitive`
  attribute makes even its `id` and `name` outputs "refer to sensitive
  values" and fails the plan. Same detection caveat as above: only a plan
  catches it.
- **Use the provider's ONE repo-wide pin shape, and check what the lock
  resolves.** Each provider's modules share a single `required_providers`
  constraint convention — some providers pin a floor (`>= 5.0.0`), others a
  major-capped range whose floor is the audited release (`~> 7.43`) because
  the next major's fields are deliberately excluded from the modeled surface
  and a lower floor could lie about what a module's arguments need; follow
  the sibling modules of the SAME provider, never mix shapes within one
  provider. Either way, a
  stale `.terraform.lock.hcl` silently freezes the resolved provider -- so
  when adding fields that need a newer provider, run `terraform init -upgrade`
  and confirm the lock file's resolved version, or `terraform validate` fails
  with "Unsupported argument" on fields the current provider genuinely has.
  Keep sibling modules of one provider on the same resolved version so
  behavior never varies per kind.
- **Enum values arrive as the FULL proto enum value name -- match them
  exactly.** The tfvars pipeline serializes enum fields as the proto value's
  declared name (e.g. `LINUX_AUTOMATIC_BY_PLATFORM`, `ASSESSMENT_IMAGE_DEFAULT`),
  including any disambiguation prefix the spec added because sibling enums in
  one file cannot share value names. A `locals.tf` comparison against a
  shortened form (`"AUTOMATIC_BY_PLATFORM"`) can never match: the ternary
  silently resolves null, the feature is silently dropped, and `terraform
  validate` passes. When writing enum maps, copy each value name verbatim from
  `spec.proto` -- and prove every conditional enum seam with a `planton tofu
  plan` whose manifest actually carries those fields (see the e2e-manifest
  rule): a plan only proves the fields it exercises.
- **The variable contract is only proven by a live run, never by `terraform
  validate`.** `validate` checks the module against itself; it cannot detect a
  `variables.tf` whose types the tfvars pipeline can never satisfy (e.g. labels/
  annotations typed as `{key,value}` objects instead of `map(string)`, or a
  `StringValueOrRef` field typed as a strict object instead of the pre-resolved
  `string` the orchestrator delivers). The current contract: metadata uses
  `optional(...)` attributes with `map(string)` labels/annotations; every
  `StringValueOrRef` spec field arrives pre-resolved as `string` (repeated ones
  as `list(string)`). When updating an older module, conform its `variables.tf`
  to this shape before anything else -- the E2E Terraform run is the proof.
- **Locals evaluate eagerly -- bound every derivation that can see an empty
  optional.** Terraform computes ALL locals on every plan, even ones consumed
  only inside a `count = 0` resource. A family derivation like
  `slice(split(".", var.spec.engine_version), 0, 2)` hard-fails the whole plan
  when `engine_version` is unpinned (split of "" yields one element; the slice
  end exceeds the list). Bound it: `slice(parts, 0, min(2, length(parts)))`.
  `tofu validate` cannot catch this (it never evaluates against real inputs) --
  it surfaced only in a live E2E deploy. The Pulumi modules are immune because
  Go helpers are called conditionally.
- **`google.protobuf.Struct` spec fields arrive in tfvars as NESTED OBJECTS,
  never JSON strings.** The generated contract types them `optional(any)` and
  the proto->tfvars converter passes the protojson value through verbatim (a
  real nested object/array/scalar). A module that treats such a field as a
  string -- `var.spec.policy != "" ? var.spec.policy : null` -- passes
  `tofu validate` (the variable is `any`) and fails only at live apply with
  "Inappropriate value for attribute: string required". The correct idiom for
  every Struct field whose provider argument wants a JSON document:
  `var.spec.policy != null ? jsonencode(var.spec.policy) : null`. Fields the
  spec models as raw JSON *strings* (e.g. an SNS `delivery_policy`) keep the
  string comparison -- check the proto type, not the field name.
- **User string content passes through the tfvars writer with HCL template
  introducers escaped.** The proto->tfvars converter emits `$${` / `%%{` for
  literal `${` / `%{` inside string values, so fields carrying IAM policy JSON
  (`${aws:PrincipalAccount}` condition variables), shell user-data, and similar
  template-looking content arrive in Terraform as literal characters. Modules
  therefore consume such fields directly -- never add escaping in HCL, and never
  avoid modeling a policy/user-data string field out of interpolation concerns.
  (Exercise one template-introducer-bearing value in the kind's E2E scenario:
  `tofu validate` never sees real tfvars, so only a rendered run proves it.)
- **The offline `tofu plan` proof: render tfvars with
  `planton tofu load-tfvars <path to e2e/manifest.yaml>` (positional arg; prints
  HCL tfvars to stdout), save to a file, then in `iac/tf/` run
  `tofu init -backend=false` + `tofu plan -input=false -refresh=false
  -var-file=<file>`.** Two credential realities: the AWS provider validates
  credentials via an STS `GetCallerIdentity` call at PLAN time, so dummy static
  keys fail the "offline" proof — a real credential chain is required even
  though `-refresh=false` with no state reads or mutates nothing. And the
  provider's AWS SDK can fail to refresh a cached SSO token that the AWS CLI
  itself accepts — when an `AWS_PROFILE` SSO profile errors with "refresh
  cached SSO token failed", export short-lived static credentials instead:
  `eval $(aws configure export-credentials --profile <profile> --format env)`.
  The proof passes when the plan renders every expected resource
  (`Plan: N to add`) with zero errors. Remove the `.terraform/`,
  `.terraform.lock.hcl`, and any plan-file artifacts afterwards (gitignored,
  but they bloat the working tree).

HANDLING OPTIONAL FIELDS WITH DEFAULTS
- Fields with `(dev.planton.shared.options.default)` are `optional` in the proto schema
- Planton middleware populates defaults BEFORE Terraform runs, so:
  - The generated `variables.tf` may show `optional(string, null)` for these fields
  - At runtime, the value will NEVER actually be null for defaulted fields
  - NO `coalesce()`, `try()`, or conditional default wrappers are needed in `locals.tf`
- In `locals.tf`, reference defaulted fields directly: `var.spec.field_name` is guaranteed non-null
- In resource definitions, use the value directly without defensive handling

HANDLING PROTO-OPTIONAL TRI-STATES (presence WITHOUT a platform default)
- A proto3 `optional` scalar WITHOUT `(options.default)` is a genuine tri-state:
  unset means "let the cloud default decide" and an explicit zero value (false/0)
  is a real user choice that must NOT collapse into "unset".
- The generated contract renders these as `optional(<type>)` with a NULL default
  (never `optional(bool, false)` / `optional(number, 0)` -- a zero default would
  silently override the cloud's own default on every resource that omitted the
  field, and diverge from the Pulumi module's nil-pointer handling).
- In the module, pass such attributes through null-aware: `var.spec.field` when
  the provider accepts null directly, or `var.spec.field == null ? null :
  tostring(var.spec.field)` for the provider's string-typed nullable-bools.
  NEVER write `field > 0 ? field : null` against a tri-state -- null > 0 is an
  evaluation error, and it erases an explicit zero. Guard comparisons with
  `coalesce(field, 0)` when a numeric test is genuinely needed.
- **HCL's `&&` does NOT short-circuit.** `obj != null && obj.attr` evaluates
  BOTH operands and errors with "Attempt to get attribute from null value"
  whenever the object is null -- and `terraform validate` cannot catch it
  (only a run with the attribute pruned does). Null-guard nested-object
  access with a ternary instead: `obj != null ? obj.attr : false`, or the
  `for_each = obj != null ? [obj] : []` dynamic-block idiom. This applies in
  EVERY expression context, not just dynamic blocks -- a `count` guard like
  `count = enabled && var.spec.block.sub != null ? 1 : 0` errors identically
  when `block` is null; nest ternaries there too:
  `count = enabled ? (var.spec.block.sub != null ? 1 : 0) : 0`. Before
  finishing, sweep the module for `&&`/`||` whose operands dereference a
  possibly-null object. When MANY flat provider attributes read off one
  nullable block (per-protocol triads, feature sub-objects), normalize the
  block to a zero-value object once in `locals.tf` --
  `try(coalesce(var.spec.block.sub), local.zero)` -- so every attribute
  expression reads it unconditionally instead of repeating null guards.
- **`can()` does NOT null-guard either.** `can(o.s3_origin) && o.s3_origin.attr`
  still errors when `s3_origin` is null: `can(o.s3_origin)` returns true (the
  expression evaluates fine, to null), and then the second operand dereferences
  null. The robust idiom for nested access behind a possibly-null parent is
  `try(obj.attr, default)` -- `try(o.s3_origin.create_oac, false)` is both the
  null guard and the default in one expression, and works in `for` filters,
  `for_each`, and resource arguments alike.
- **`coalesce(x, "")` ERRORS when every argument is empty.** HCL's `coalesce`
  requires at least one non-null, NON-EMPTY-STRING argument, so
  `coalesce(field, "") != ""` fails at plan time exactly when the field is
  absent -- the case it was meant to guard. Generator-flattened
  `StringValueOrRef` fields (and every `optional(string, "")` contract
  attribute) are never null -- they carry the contract default `""` -- so
  test them with a plain `field != ""`. Reserve `coalesce` for genuinely
  nullable attributes with a non-empty fallback (e.g.
  `coalesce(nullable_enum, "OPTIONAL")`), and only `tofu plan` with real
  tfvars proves the difference (`tofu validate` cannot).
- Example:
  ```hcl
  # CORRECT: Planton guarantees content_type is populated (default: application/octet-stream)
  content_type = each.value.content_type

  # WRONG: Unnecessary defensive default - Planton already handled this
  content_type = coalesce(each.value.content_type, "application/octet-stream")
  ```
- See the reference component `iac/tf/` directories named in TOOLS for worked examples

HANDLING RECURSIVE PROTO MESSAGES (self-referential statement trees)
- When a spec message references itself (e.g. WAFv2 AND/OR/NOT statements, or any
  nested tree whose descriptor cycle would hang the variables generator), the
  generator detects the cycle on the current walk path and collapses that subtree
  to bare `any` in `variables.tf`.
- Terraform cannot express `list(any)` or `map(any)` when element types differ
  across entries (heterogeneous object shapes under one repeated field). When a
  list or map's element type contains free-form `any`, the generator emits the
  **entire attribute** as bare `any` (`TFFreeFormList` / `TFFreeFormMap`), not
  `list(object({...}))` with an `any` leaf -- otherwise `tofu plan` fails with
  "element types must all match for conversion to list".
- The module must serialize such trees itself (typically `rule_json` / `jsonencode`
  in HCL, or a Go builder in Pulumi) rather than expecting typed nested Terraform
  resources for every statement variant. Add a unit test on the serializer and a
  regression test in `variablestf_test.go` when introducing a new recursive spec.
- **NO 64-bit integers inside an `any`-typed subtree that feeds a JSON provider
  argument.** protojson stringifies int64/uint64 ("16384", not 16384) — the
  typed-contract path never notices, but a provider that unmarshals the
  serialized JSON into typed Go structs rejects it at APPLY time ("cannot
  unmarshal string into ... of type int64"), and Pulumi's Go builder (which
  marshals real ints) silently diverges. Type such fields int32/uint32 when the
  domain fits (it almost always does — sizes cap at int32 max, ASNs are uint32);
  only a live Terraform run catches the mismatch, never `tofu validate`.